{"author":{"id":"1832e4168d45d4ef31531146dbc743bb950a5dd543b15fd8e3a076fd976bfdef"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790976402,"nonce":"a5DALiElQthAAODyvoshUJfzzlw=","title":"the wire can read a GPUBuffer's mapped range but not write into it","message":"GPUBuffer.getMappedRange returns an ArrayBuffer. The wire turns that into a reply: the agent copies the range into the guest's reply buffer (opcode 77 in xgpu's browser wire). That serves a buffer mapped for reading. A buffer mapped for writing, or created with mappedAtCreation, is filled by writing into that same ArrayBuffer, and nothing on the wire can carry the guest's bytes there: arguments are only what the IDL operation takes, and getMappedRange takes an offset and a size.\n\nFound 2026-10-02 writing xgpu's buffer_copy_in for the web (xgpu git-bug 106cf53fe7b624d802a6e53bffdb0a23e9ef7da8d3e4163cb6a461cb047b05ac): GpuBuffer.copyIn writes into a range mapped for writing or at creation, and the web cannot do it. queue.writeBuffer is no substitute; WebGPU rejects it on a mapped buffer.\n\nWhat would do: for an operation whose result is ArrayBuffer, a second encoder method that takes the guest's bytes (the existing `Bytes` argument, address and length) along with the operation's arguments, and has the agent run `new Uint8Array(self.getMappedRange(a0, a1)).set(bytes)`, replying with success or the exception's message.","files":null}]}